<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch10_P01_Lost_solution</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>A student claims that the equation in line 1 has only one solution, \(x = {@claimed@}\), and argues as follows.</p>
<table>
<tr><td>1</td><td>\({@eqn@}\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>[[if test='is(tmpl = 1)']]\(x(x - {@pp@}) = {@qq@}(x - {@pp@})\)[[else]]\(x - {@pp@} = {@qq@}\)[[/if]]</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>[[if test='is(tmpl = 1)']]\(x = {@qq@}\)[[else]]\(x = {@pp + qq@}\)[[/if]]</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Check: when \(x = {@claimed@}\), both sides of line 1 equal {@chk@}.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>So \(x = {@claimed@}\) is the only solution.</td></tr>
</table>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Which is the first line that does not follow from the lines before it?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>Give a solution of the equation in line 1 that is not \(x = {@claimed@}\).</p>
<div>\(x =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
<li><p>Why does that line not follow? (Optional: this box is not marked and not saved.)</p>
<div>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Line {@errline@} divides both sides by \(x - {@pp@}\). That is valid only when \(x - {@pp@} \neq 0\), so the argument has quietly assumed that \(x \neq {@pp@}\). But \(x = {@pp@}\) is a solution: both sides of line 1 are 0 there. So line {@errline@} is the first line that does not follow; the lines before it are correct, and the check in line 4 shows only that \(x = {@claimed@}\) is one solution, not that it is the only one.</p>
<p>The safe method is to collect everything on one side and factorise, rather than divide. This gives [[if test='is(tmpl = 1)']]\[(x - {@pp@})(x - {@qq@}) = 0,\][[else]]\[(x - {@pp@})(x - {@pp + qq@}) = 0,\][[/if]] so the equation has two solutions, \(x = {@pp@}\) and \(x = {@claimed@}\).</p>
<p>This is the flaw in the proof that \(2 = 1\), in a form you may meet when solving equations. There the expression divided by, \(a - b\), was always 0; here it is 0 for one value of \(x\), and that value is lost.</p>
<p>A reason you might have written: <q>Line {@errline@} divides by \(x - {@pp@}\), which is 0 when \(x = {@pp@}\).</q></p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1000000</penalty>
